"use strict";(self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith=self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith||[]).push([[17573],{617573:e=>{e.exports="# Google Analytics 4 Raw Data\n\n## Guidelines\n\n> **IMPORTANT** — You help the user **set up Google Analytics 4 Raw Data ingestion in Databricks**. **Do not write data to BigQuery or otherwise modify the customer's Google Cloud project** (no creates, updates, deletes, or configuration changes via APIs, jobs, or scripts). **Read-only** operations are **expected and appropriate** here—including listing datasets and tables, schema inspection, permission verification, and **non-mutating queries that only test accessibility** without changing project state. If something would alter the BigQuery project, tell the user to do it in Google Cloud Console—not via assistant-driven mutations.\n\n## Setting Up Google Analytics 4 Raw Data\n\n### Prerequisites\n\nBefore creating the pipeline:\n\n1. **Enable GA4 BigQuery export** in Google Analytics Admin settings — without this, no `analytics_*` datasets exist in BigQuery.\n2. **Create a service account** in the Google Cloud project and grant it these IAM roles:\n   - **BigQuery Data Viewer** (`bigquery.datasets.get`, `bigquery.tables.list`, `bigquery.tables.get`, `bigquery.tables.getData`)\n   - **BigQuery Job User** (`bigquery.jobs.create`)\n   - **BigQuery Read Session User** (`bigquery.readsessions.create`, `bigquery.readsessions.getData`) — this role is commonly missed and causes runtime `NO_BQ_READ_SESSION_USER_ACCESS` failures even when all other permissions are correct\n3. **Grant the service account access** to the BigQuery dataset (or at project level to cover all GA4 properties).\n\n### Configuration Notes\n\nThe connector reads from a BigQuery dataset named `analytics_{property_id}`. Extract from the pipeline spec:\n\n- `connectionName` from `ingestion_definition.connection_name`\n- `datasetId` from `objects[].table.source_schema` (e.g. `analytics_123456789`)\n- `tableId` from `objects[].table.source_table` (e.g. `events_20240101`)\n\n## Diagnosing Google Analytics 4 Raw Data Connection\n\n### Diagnostic Flow\n\n1. **Check BigQuery access** — call `ga4_listDatasets`. If auth error, credentials are invalid or expired. If 403, service account lacks BigQuery Data Viewer role. Look for datasets matching `analytics_XXXXXX`.\n2. **List tables** — call `ga4_listTablesInDataset` for the GA4 dataset. Look for `events_*`, `events_intraday_*`, `events_fresh_*`, `users_*`, `pseudonymous_users_*` tables.\n3. **Check table access** — call `ga4_testTableAccess`. If 403, user lacks permissions. If 404, table doesn't exist. Check `elapsed_seconds` — times >60s indicate performance issues.\n4. **Check permissions** — call `ga4_checkBigqueryPermissions` to verify all required IAM permissions including `readsessions`. Missing `readsessions` causes `NO_BQ_READ_SESSION_USER_ACCESS` even if the Jobs API works.\n5. **Check metadata** — call `ga4_getTableMetadata` to inspect schema and row count. 0 rows means GA4 export may not be configured or sending data.\n\n## Source Reference\n\n### GA4 Table Types\n\nGA4 exports create several table types in BigQuery, each serving a different analytical purpose:\n\n| Table pattern                 | Contents                                     | Notes                                          |\n| ----------------------------- | -------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------- |\n| `events_YYYYMMDD`             | Daily event export                           | Primary table for most analytics               |\n| `events_intraday_YYYYMMDD`    | Streaming events, updated throughout the day | Replaced by the daily table after the day ends |\n| `events_fresh_YYYYMMDD`       | Continuous export (fresher than daily)       | Only available with GA4 360                    |\n| `users_YYYYMMDD`              | User property snapshots                      | Useful for user-level analysis                 |\n| `pseudonymous_users_YYYYMMDD` | Pseudonymous user data                       | Privacy-compliant alternative to users table   |\n\nMost users should start with `events_*` tables. For near-real-time needs, add `events_intraday_*`.\n\n## Common Pitfalls\n\n- GA4 BigQuery export must be enabled in Google Analytics Admin settings — without it, no `analytics_*` datasets exist and all diagnostics will fail with dataset-not-found errors\n- **BigQuery Read Session User role is commonly missed** — all other permission checks pass, but the pipeline fails at runtime with `NO_BQ_READ_SESSION_USER_ACCESS`\n"}}]);
